## an5.76, continued
an5.76:14.10 | S: He goes to the monastery and tells the monks: | T: Going to the monastery, he tells the monks,
an5.76:14.11 | S: ‘Reverends, I am overcome with lust, mired in lust. I am unable to keep up the spiritual life. | T: ‘Friends, I am assailed by lust, overcome by lust. I can’t continue in the holy life.
an5.76:14.12 | S: I declare my inability to continue training. I resign it and will return to a lesser life.’ | T: Declaring my weakness in the training, renouncing the training, I will return to the lower life.’
an5.76:15.1 | S: His spiritual companions advise and instruct him: | T: “Then his companions in the holy life admonish & instruct him,
an5.76:15.2 | S: ‘Reverend, the Buddha says that sensual pleasures give little gratification and much suffering and distress, and they are all the more full of drawbacks. | T: ‘Friend, the Blessed One has said that sensual pleasures are of little satisfaction, of much stress, much despair, & greater drawbacks.
an5.76:15.3 | S: With the simile of a skeleton … | T: The Blessed One has compared sensual pleasures to a chain of bones—of much stress, much despair, & greater drawbacks.
an5.76:15.4 | S: a scrap of meat … a grass torch … a pit of glowing coals … a dream … borrowed goods … fruit on a tree … a butcher’s knife and chopping board … swords and spears … a cobra’s head, the Buddha says that sensual pleasures give little gratification and much suffering and distress, and they are all the more full of drawbacks. | T: He has compared sensual pleasures to a lump of flesh… a grass torch… a pit of glowing embers… a dream… borrowed goods… the fruits of a tree… a slaughterhouse… spears & swords… a poisonous snake—of much stress, much despair, & greater drawbacks.
an5.76:15.5 | S: Be happy with the spiritual life. | T: Find delight, friend, in the holy life.
an5.76:15.6 | S: Venerable, please don’t declare your inability to continue training, resign it and return to a lesser life.’ | T: Don’t declare your weakness in the training, renounce the training, or return to the lower life.’
an5.76:16.1 | S: When thus advised and instructed by his spiritual companions, he says: | T: “Thus admonished & instructed by his companions in the holy life, he responds,
an5.76:16.2 | S: ‘I’ll make an attempt, reverends, I’ll struggle, I’ll be happy. | T: ‘I will strive, friends. I will remember. I will find delight in the holy life.
an5.76:16.3 | S: I won’t now declare my inability to continue training, resign it and return to a lesser life.’ | T: I won’t yet declare my weakness in the training, renounce the training, or return to the lower life.’
an5.76:17.1 | S: I say that this individual is like the warrior who recovers from his injuries while in the care of his relatives. | T: This individual, I tell you, is like the warrior who—taking his sword & shield, strapping on his bow & quiver—goes down into the thick of battle. There in the battle he strives & makes effort. But while he is striving & making an effort, his opponents wound him. He gets carried out and taken to his relatives, who nurse him and care for him, and he recovers from his injury.
an5.76:17.2 | S: Some individuals are like that. | T: Some individuals are like this.
an5.76:17.3 | S: This is the fourth individual similar to a warrior found among the monks. | T: This is the fourth type of warrior-like individual who can be found existing among the monks.
an5.76:18.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant lives supported by a town or village. | T: [5] “And further, there is the case of the monk who dwells in dependence on a certain village or town.
an5.76:18.2 | S: He robes up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, enters a village or town, guarding body, speech, and mind, establishing mindfulness, and restraining the sense faculties. | T: Early in the morning, having adjusted his lower robe and carrying his bowl & outer robe, he goes into the village or town for alms—with his body, speech, & mind protected, with mindfulness established, with his sense faculties guarded.
an5.76:18.3 | S: Seeing a sight with his eyes, he doesn’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: On seeing a form with the eye, does not grasp at any theme or particulars by which—
an5.76:18.4 | S: If the faculty of sight were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, he practices restraint, protecting the faculty of sight, and achieving its restraint. | T: if he were to dwell without restraint over the faculty of the eye—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail him. He practices with restraint. He guards the faculty of the eye. He achieves restraint with regard to the faculty of the eye.
an5.76:18.5 | S: Hearing a sound with his ears … | T: “On hearing a sound with the ear.…
an5.76:18.6 | S: Smelling an odor with his nose … | T: “On smelling an aroma with the nose.…
an5.76:18.7 | S: Tasting a flavor with his tongue … | T: “On tasting a flavor with the tongue.…
an5.76:18.8 | S: Encountering a touch with his body … | T: “On touching a tactile sensation with the body.…
an5.76:18.9 | S: Knowing an idea with his mind, he doesn’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: “On cognizing an idea with the intellect, he does not grasp at any theme or particulars by which—
an5.76:18.10 | S: If the faculty of mind were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, he practices restraint, protecting the faculty of mind, and achieving its restraint. | T: if he were to dwell without restraint over the faculty of the intellect—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail him. He practices with restraint. He guards the faculty of the intellect. He achieves restraint with regard to the faculty of the intellect.
an5.76:18.11 | S: Then after the meal, on his return from almsround, | T: “Returning from his alms round, after his meal,
an5.76:18.12 | S: he frequents a secluded lodging—a wilderness, the root of a tree, a hill, a ravine, a mountain cave, a charnel ground, a forest, the open air, a heap of straw. | T: he resorts to a secluded dwelling place: the wilderness, the foot of a tree, a mountain, a glen, a hillside cave, a charnel ground, a forest grove, the open air, a haystack.
an5.76:18.13 | S: Gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, he sits down cross-legged, sets his body straight, and brings mindfulness to the present. | T: Having gone to the wilderness, the foot of a tree, or an empty building, he sits down, crosses his legs, holds his body erect, and brings mindfulness to the fore.
an5.76:18.14 | S: He gives up these five hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom. | T: “Abandoning covetousness with regard to the world, he dwells with an awareness devoid of covetousness. He cleanses his mind of covetousness. Abandoning ill will & anger, he dwells with an awareness devoid of ill will, sympathetic with the welfare of all living beings. He cleanses his mind of ill will & anger. Abandoning sloth & drowsiness, he dwells with an awareness devoid of sloth & drowsiness, mindful, alert, percipient of light. He cleanses his mind of sloth & drowsiness. Abandoning restlessness & anxiety, he dwells undisturbed, his mind inwardly stilled. He cleanses his mind of restlessness & anxiety. Abandoning uncertainty, he dwells having crossed over uncertainty, with no perplexity with regard to skillful qualities. He cleanses his mind of uncertainty. “Having abandoned these five hindrances, corruptions of awareness that weaken discernment,
an5.76:18.15 | S: Then, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, he enters and remains in the first absorption … second absorption … third absorption … fourth absorption. | T: then—quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—he enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation. With the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, he enters & remains in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance. With the fading of rapture, he remains equanimous, mindful, & alert, and senses pleasure with the body. He enters & remains in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding.’ With the abandoning of pleasure & pain—as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress—he enters & remains in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain.
an5.76:19.1 | S: When his mind has become immersed in samādhi like this—purified, bright, flawless, rid of corruptions, pliable, workable, steady, and imperturbable—he extends it toward knowledge of the ending of defilements. | T: “With his mind thus concentrated, purified, & bright, unblemished, free from defects, pliant, malleable, steady, & attained to imperturbability, he directs and inclines it to the knowledge of the ending of the effluents.
an5.76:19.2 | S: He truly understands: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. He truly understands: ‘These are defilements’ … ‘This is the origin of defilements’ … ‘This is the cessation of defilements’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of defilements’. Knowing and seeing like this, his mind is freed from the defilements of sensuality, desire to be reborn, and ignorance. When he’s freed, he knows he’s freed. | T: He discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress … This is the origination of stress … This is the cessation of stress … This is the way leading to the cessation of stress … These are effluents … This is the origination of effluents … This is the cessation of effluents … This is the way leading to the cessation of effluents.’ His heart, thus knowing, thus seeing, is released from the effluent of sensuality, released from the effluent of becoming, released from the effluent of ignorance. With release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released.’
an5.76:19.3 | S: He understands: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’
an5.76:20.1 | S: I say that this individual is like the warrior who dons his sword and shield, fastens his bow and arrows, and plunges into the thick of battle. He wins victory in battle, establishing himself as foremost in battle. | T: “This individual, I tell you, is like the warrior who—taking his sword & shield, strapping on his bow & quiver—goes down into the thick of battle. On winning the battle, victorious in battle, he comes out at the very head of the battle.
an5.76:20.2 | S: Some individuals are like that. | T: Some individuals are like this.
an5.76:20.3 | S: This is the fifth individual similar to a warrior found among the monks. | T: This is the fifth type of warrior-like individual who can be found existing among the monks.
an5.76:20.4 | S: These five individuals similar to warriors are found among the monks.” | T: “These are the five warrior-like individuals who can be found existing among the monks.”
an5.76:20.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.77
an5.77:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, seeing these five future perils is quite enough for a wilderness mendicant to meditate diligently, keenly, and resolutely for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized. | T: “Monks, these five future dangers are just enough, when considered, for a monk living in the wilderness—heedful, ardent, & resolute—to live for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized.
an5.77:2.1 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an5.77:2.2 | S: Firstly, a wilderness mendicant reflects: | T: “There is the case where a monk living in the wilderness reminds himself of this:
an5.77:2.3 | S: ‘Currently I’m living alone in a wilderness. | T: ‘I am now living alone in the wilderness.
an5.77:2.4 | S: While living here alone | T: While I am living alone in the wilderness,
an5.77:2.5 | S: I might get bitten by a snake, a scorpion, or a centipede. | T: a snake might bite me, a scorpion might sting me, a centipede might bite me.
an5.77:2.6 | S: And if I died from that it would be an obstacle to my progress. | T: That would be how my death would come about. That would be an obstruction for me.
an5.77:2.7 | S: I’d better rouse up energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized.’ | T: So let me make an effort for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized.’
an5.77:2.8 | S: This is the first future peril … | T: “This is the first future danger that is just enough, when considered, for a monk living in the wilderness—heedful, ardent, & resolute—to live for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized.
an5.77:3.1 | S: Furthermore, a wilderness mendicant reflects: | T: “And further, the monk living in the wilderness reminds himself of this:
an5.77:3.2 | S: ‘Currently I’m living alone in a wilderness. | T: ‘I am now living alone in the wilderness.
an5.77:3.3 | S: While living here alone | T: While I am living alone in the wilderness,
an5.77:3.4 | S: I might stumble off a cliff, or get food poisoning, or suffer a disturbance of bile, phlegm, or piercing winds. | T: stumbling, I might fall; my food, digested, might trouble me; my bile might be provoked, my phlegm… piercing wind forces (in the body) might be provoked.
an5.77:3.5 | S: And if I died from that it would be an obstacle to my progress. | T: That would be how my death would come about. That would be an obstruction for me.
an5.77:3.6 | S: I’d better rouse up energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized.’ | T: So let me make an effort for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized.’
