## an5.78
an5.78:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, seeing these five future perils is quite enough for a mendicant to meditate diligently, keenly, and resolutely for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized. | T: “Monks, these five future dangers are just enough, when considered, for a monk—heedful, ardent, & resolute—to live for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized.
an5.78:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an5.78:1.3 | S: A mendicant reflects: | T: “There is the case where a monk reminds himself of this:
an5.78:1.4 | S: ‘Currently I’m a youth, young, with pristine black hair, blessed with youth, in the prime of life. | T: ‘At present I am young, black-haired, endowed with the blessings of youth in the first stage of life.
an5.78:1.5 | S: But there will come a time when this body is struck with old age. | T: The time will come, though, when this body is beset by old age.
an5.78:1.6 | S: When you’re old, overcome by old age, it’s not easy to focus on the instructions of the Buddhas, and it’s not easy to frequent remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest. | T: When one is overcome with old age & decay, it is not easy to pay attention to the Buddha’s teachings. It is not easy to reside in isolated forest or wilderness dwellings.
an5.78:1.7 | S: Before that unlikable, undesirable, and disagreeable thing happens, | T: Before this unwelcome, disagreeable, displeasing thing happens,
an5.78:1.8 | S: I’d better preempt it by rousing up energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized. That way, when it happens, I’ll live comfortably even though I’m old.’ | T: let me first make an effort for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized, so that—endowed with that Dhamma—I will live in peace even when old.’
an5.78:1.9 | S: This is the first future peril … | T: “This is the first future danger that is just enough, when considered, for a monk—heedful, ardent, & resolute—to live for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized.
an5.78:2.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant reflects: | T: “And further, the monk reminds himself of this:
an5.78:2.2 | S: ‘Currently, I’m rarely ill or unwell. My stomach digests well, being neither too hot nor too cold, but just right, and fit for meditation. | T: ‘At present I am free from illness & discomfort, endowed with good digestion: not too cold, not too hot, of medium strength & tolerance.
an5.78:2.3 | S: But there will come a time when this body is struck with sickness. | T: The time will come, though, when this body is beset with illness.
an5.78:2.4 | S: When you’re sick, overcome by sickness, it’s not easy to focus on the instructions of the Buddhas, and it’s not easy to frequent remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest. | T: When one is overcome with illness, it is not easy to pay attention to the Buddha’s teachings. It is not easy to reside in isolated forest or wilderness dwellings.
an5.78:2.5 | S: Before that unlikable, undesirable, and disagreeable thing happens, | T: Before this unwelcome, disagreeable, displeasing thing happens,
an5.78:2.6 | S: I’d better preempt it by rousing up energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized. That way, when it happens, I’ll live comfortably even though I’m sick.’ | T: let me first make an effort for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized, so that—endowed with that Dhamma—I will live in peace even when ill.’
an5.78:2.7 | S: This is the second future peril … | T: “This is the second future danger that is just enough, when considered, for a monk—heedful, ardent, & resolute—to live for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized.
an5.78:3.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant reflects: | T: “And further, the monk reminds himself of this:
an5.78:3.2 | S: ‘Currently, there’s plenty of food, a good harvest, so it’s easy to get almsfood, and easy to keep going by collecting alms. | T: ‘At present food is plentiful, alms are easy to come by. It is easy to maintain oneself by gleanings & patronage.
an5.78:3.3 | S: But there will come a time of famine, a bad harvest, when it’s hard to get almsfood, and not easy to keep going by collecting alms. | T: The time will come, though, when there is famine: Food is scarce, alms are hard to come by, and it is not easy to maintain oneself by gleanings & patronage.
an5.78:3.4 | S: In a time of famine, people move to where there’s plenty of food, | T: When there is famine, people will congregate where food is plentiful.
an5.78:3.5 | S: where they live crowded and cramped together. | T: There they will live packed & crowded together.
an5.78:3.6 | S: When you live crowded and cramped together, it’s not easy to focus on the instructions of the Buddhas, and it’s not easy to frequent remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest. | T: When one is living packed & crowded together, it is not easy to pay attention to the Buddha’s teachings. It is not easy to reside in isolated forest or wilderness dwellings.
an5.78:3.7 | S: Before that unlikable, undesirable, and disagreeable thing happens, | T: Before this unwelcome, disagreeable, displeasing thing happens,
an5.78:3.8 | S: I’d better preempt it by rousing up energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized. That way, when it happens, I’ll live comfortably even though there’s a famine.’ | T: let me first make an effort for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized, so that—endowed with that Dhamma—I will live in peace even when there is famine.’
