## an5.7
an5.7:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, sentient beings are mostly charmed by sensual pleasures. | T: “For the most part, monks, beings are led astray by sensuality.
an5.7:1.2 | S: When a gentleman has abandoned the scythe and flail and gone forth from the lay life to homelessness, they’re qualified to be called ‘a faithful renunciate from a good family’. | T: When a son of good family, leaving behind the sickle & carrying pole, has gone forth from the household life into homelessness, he can be called one who has gone forth through conviction.
an5.7:1.3 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an5.7:1.4 | S: Because a youth can get sensual pleasures of this kind or that. | T: Because sensual pleasures, whether of this or that type, are obtainable by a youth.
an5.7:1.5 | S: Now, all sensual pleasures are just reckoned as ‘sensual pleasures’, regardless of whether they’re inferior, average, or superior. | T: Inferior sensual pleasures, middling sensual pleasures, and superior sensual pleasures are all simply reckoned as ‘sensual pleasures.’
an5.7:1.6 | S: Suppose there was a little baby boy who, because of his nursemaid’s negligence, puts a stick or stone in his mouth. | T: “It’s just as if a stupid baby boy, lying on its back, might—through the heedlessness of his nurse—take a stick or a piece of gravel into its mouth.
an5.7:1.7 | S: The nursemaid would very quickly notice | T: The nurse would attend to him right away.
an5.7:1.8 | S: and try to take it out. | T: Attending to him right away, she would take it out right away.
an5.7:1.9 | S: If that didn’t work, she’d cradle his head with her left hand, and take it out using a hooked finger of her right hand, even if it drew blood. | T: If she couldn’t take it out right away, then holding his head in her left hand and crooking a finger of her right, she would take it out, even if it meant drawing blood.
an5.7:1.10 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an5.7:1.11 | S: I admit she’d know, ‘This will distress the child, | T: There would be some injury to the boy—
an5.7:1.12 | S: there’s no denying.’ | T: I don’t say that there wouldn’t—
an5.7:1.13 | S: Still, it should be done by a nursemaid who wants what’s best for him, out of kindness and sympathy. | T: but the nurse should act in that way out of sympathy, wanting his well-being and seeking his benefit.
an5.7:1.14 | S: And when the boy has grown up and has enough sense, his nursemaid would not be concerned about him, thinking: | T: But when the boy has grown and has enough discernment, the nurse can be unconcerned about him, [thinking,]
an5.7:1.15 | S: ‘The boy can look after himself. He won’t be negligent.’ | T: ‘The boy can now look after himself. He won’t be heedless.’
an5.7:2.1 | S: In the same way, I still need to look after a mendicant who hasn’t finished developing faith, conscience, prudence, energy, and wisdom regarding skillful qualities. | T: “In the same way, as long as a monk is not developed in conviction with regard to skillful qualities, not developed in shame with regard to skillful qualities, not developed in compunction with regard to skillful qualities, not developed in persistence with regard to skillful qualities, and not developed in discernment with regard to skillful qualities, I have to look after him.
an5.7:2.2 | S: But when a mendicant has finished developing faith, conscience, prudence, energy, and wisdom regarding skillful qualities, I need not be concerned, thinking: | T: But when he is developed in conviction with regard to skillful qualities, developed in shame with regard to skillful qualities, developed in compunction with regard to skillful qualities, developed in persistence with regard to skillful qualities, and developed in discernment with regard to skillful qualities, then I can be unconcerned about him, [thinking,]
an5.7:2.3 | S: ‘They can look after themselves. They won’t be negligent.’” | T: ‘The monk can now look after himself. He won’t be heedless.’”
an5.7:2.4 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.96
an5.96:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a mendicant cultivating mindfulness of breathing who has five things will soon penetrate the unshakable. | T: “Endowed with five qualities, a monk pursuing mindfulness of breathing will in no long time penetrate the Unprovoked (release).
an5.96:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an5.96:1.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant has few obligations and duties, and is unburdensome and contented with life’s necessities. | T: “He is a person who imposes only a little (on others): one of few duties & projects, easy to support, easily contented with the requisites of life.
an5.96:1.4 | S: They eat little, not devoted to filling their stomach. | T: “He is a person who eats only a little food, committed to not indulging his stomach.
an5.96:1.5 | S: They are rarely drowsy, and are dedicated to wakefulness. | T: “He is a person of only a little sloth, committed to wakefulness.
an5.96:1.6 | S: They’re very learned, remembering and keeping what they’ve learned. These teachings are good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased, describing a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. They are very learned in such teachings, remembering them, rehearsing them, mentally scrutinizing them, and penetrating them theoretically. | T: “He is a person of much learning, who has retained what he heard, has stored what he has heard. Whatever teachings are admirable in the beginning, admirable in the middle, admirable in the end, that—in their meaning & expression—proclaim the holy life that is entirely complete & pure: those he has listened to often, retained, discussed, accumulated, examined with his mind, and well-penetrated in terms of his views.
an5.96:1.7 | S: They review the extent of their mind’s freedom. | T: “He reflects on the mind as it is released.
an5.96:1.8 | S: A mendicant cultivating mindfulness of breathing who has these five things will soon penetrate the unshakable.” | T: “Endowed with these five qualities, a monk pursuing mindfulness of breathing will in no long time penetrate the Unprovoked.”
an5.96:1.9 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.97
an5.97:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a mendicant developing mindfulness of breathing who has five things will soon penetrate the unshakable. | T: “Endowed with five qualities, a monk pursuing mindfulness of breathing will in no long time penetrate the Unprovoked (release).
an5.97:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an5.97:1.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant has few concerns and duties, and is unburdensome and contented with life’s necessities. | T: “He is a person who imposes only a little (on others): one of few duties & projects, easy to support, easily contented with the requisites of life.
an5.97:1.4 | S: They eat little, not devoted to filling their stomach. | T: “He is a person who eats only a little food, committed to not indulging his stomach.
an5.97:1.5 | S: They are rarely drowsy, and are dedicated to wakefulness. | T: “He is a person of only a little sloth, committed to wakefulness.
an5.97:1.6 | S: They get to take part in talk about self-effacement that helps open the heart, when they want, without trouble or difficulty. That is, | T: “He gets to hear at will, easily & without difficulty, talk that is truly sobering & conducive to the opening of awareness:
an5.97:1.7 | S: talk about fewness of wishes, contentment, seclusion, keeping your distance, arousing energy, ethics, immersion, wisdom, freedom, and the knowledge and vision of freedom. | T: talk on modesty, contentment, seclusion, non-entanglement, arousing persistence, virtue, concentration, discernment, release, and the knowledge & vision of release.
an5.97:1.8 | S: They review the extent of their mind’s freedom. | T: “He reflects on the mind as it is released.
an5.97:1.9 | S: A mendicant developing mindfulness of breathing who has these five things will soon penetrate the unshakable.” | T: “Endowed with these five qualities, a monk pursuing mindfulness of breathing will in no long time penetrate the Unprovoked.”
an5.97:1.10 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

