## an5.99
an5.99:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, toward evening the lion, king of beasts, emerges from his den, | T: “Monks, the lion, the king of beasts, leaves his lair in the evening.
an5.99:1.2 | S: yawns, | T: Having left his lair, he stretches himself.
an5.99:1.3 | S: surveys the four quarters, | T: Having stretched himself, he looks all around the four directions.
an5.99:1.4 | S: and roars his lion’s roar three times. | T: Having looked all around the four directions, he roars his lion’s roar three times.
an5.99:1.5 | S: Then he sets out on the hunt. | T: Having roared his lion’s roar three times, he heads out for game.
an5.99:1.6 | S: If he strikes an elephant, he does it carefully, not carelessly. | T: When he gives a blow to an elephant, he gives the blow attentively, not inattentively.
an5.99:1.7 | S: If he strikes a buffalo … | T: When he gives a blow to a buffalo…
an5.99:1.8 | S: a cow … | T: to a cow…
an5.99:1.9 | S: a leopard … | T: to a leopard, he gives the blow attentively, not inattentively.
an5.99:1.10 | S: or any smaller creatures—even a hare or a cat—he does it carefully, not carelessly. | T: When he gives a blow to small animals, even to hares & cats, he gives the blow attentively, not inattentively.
an5.99:1.11 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an5.99:1.12 | S: Thinking: ‘May I not lose my way.’ | T: [He thinks,] ‘May the skill I’ve acquired not be lost.’
an5.99:2.1 | S: ‘Lion’ is a term for the Realized One, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha. | T: “‘Lion,’ monks, is a synonym for the Tathāgata, worthy & rightly self-awakened.
an5.99:2.2 | S: When the Realized One teaches Dhamma to an assembly, this is his lion’s roar. | T: When the Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma to his assembly, that is his lion’s roar.
an5.99:2.3 | S: When the Realized One teaches the monks … | T: When the Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma to monks, he teaches the Dhamma attentively, not inattentively.
an5.99:2.4 | S: nuns … | T: When the Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma to nuns…
an5.99:2.5 | S: laymen … | T: to male lay followers…
an5.99:2.6 | S: laywomen … | T: to female lay followers, he teaches the Dhamma attentively, not inattentively.
an5.99:2.7 | S: or ordinary people—even food-carriers and hunters—he teaches them carefully, not carelessly. | T: When the Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma to run-of-the-mill people—even to beggars & hunters of fowl—he teaches the Dhamma attentively, not inattentively.
an5.99:2.8 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an5.99:2.9 | S: Because the Realized One has respect and reverence for the teaching.” | T: The Tathāgata is one who respects the Dhamma, reveres the Dhamma.”
an5.99:2.10 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.102
an6.102:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, seeing six benefits is quite enough to establish the perception of impermanence in all conditions without qualification. | T: “In seeing six rewards, it’s enough for a monk to establish the perception of inconstancy with regard to all fabrications without exception.
an6.102:1.2 | S: What six? | T: Which six?
an6.102:1.3 | S: ‘All conditions will appear to me as transient.’ ‘My mind will not delight anywhere in the world.’ ‘My mind will rise above the whole world.’ ‘My heart will incline to extinguishment.’ ‘My fetters will be given up.’ ‘I will achieve the ultimate goal of the ascetic life.’ | T: ‘All fabrications will appear as unstable. My mind will not delight in any world. My mind will rise above every world. My heart will be inclined to unbinding. My fetters will go to their abandoning. I’ll be endowed with the foremost qualities of the contemplative life.’
an6.102:1.4 | S: Seeing these six benefits is quite enough to establish the perception of impermanence in all conditions without qualification.” | T: “In seeing these six rewards, it’s enough for a monk to establish the perception of inconstancy with regard to all fabrications without exception.”
an6.102:1.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.103
an6.103:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, seeing six benefits is quite enough to establish the perception of suffering in all conditions without qualification. | T: “In seeing six rewards, it’s enough for a monk to establish the perception of stress with regard to all fabrications without exception.
an6.103:1.2 | S: What six? | T: Which six?
an6.103:1.3 | S: ‘Perception of disillusionment will be established in me for all conditions, like a killer with a drawn sword.’ | T: ‘The perception of disenchantment will be established within me with regard to all fabrications, like a murderer with a drawn sword.
an6.103:1.4 | S: ‘My mind will rise above the whole world.’ ‘I will see extinguishment as peaceful.’ ‘My underlying tendencies will be uprooted.’ ‘I will fulfill my duty.’ ‘I will have served my Teacher with love.’ | T: My mind will rise above every world. I’ll become one who sees peace in unbinding. My obsessions will go to their destruction. I’ll be one who has completed his task. The Teacher will have been served with goodwill.’
an6.103:1.5 | S: Seeing these six benefits is quite enough to establish the perception of suffering in all conditions without qualification.” | T: “In seeing these six rewards, it’s enough for a monk to establish the perception of stress with regard to all fabrications without exception.”
an6.103:1.6 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.104
an6.104:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, seeing six benefits is quite enough to establish the perception of not-self in all things without qualification. | T: “In seeing six rewards, it’s enough for a monk to establish the perception of not-self with regard to all phenomena without exception.
an6.104:1.2 | S: What six? | T: Which six?
an6.104:1.3 | S: ‘I will not be determined by anything in the world.’ ‘My I-making will stop.’ ‘My mine-making will stop.’ ‘I will have distinct knowledge.’ ‘I will clearly see causes and the phenomena that arise from causes.’ | T: ‘I won’t be fashioned in connection with any world. My I-making will be stopped. My my-making will be stopped. I’ll be endowed with uncommon knowledge. I’ll become one who rightly sees cause, along with causally-originated phenomena.’
an6.104:1.4 | S: Seeing these six benefits is quite enough to establish the perception of not-self in all things without qualification.” | T: “In seeing these six rewards, it’s enough for a monk to establish the perception of not-self with regard to all phenomena without exception.”
an6.104:1.5 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.12
an6.12:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these six warm-hearted qualities make for fondness and respect, conducing to inclusion, harmony, and unity, without dispute. | T: “Monks, these six are conditions that are conducive to amiability, that engender feelings of endearment, engender feelings of respect, leading to a sense of fellowship, a lack of disputes, harmony, & a state of unity.
an6.12:1.2 | S: What six? | T: Which six?
an6.12:1.3 | S: Firstly, a mendicant consistently treats their spiritual companions with bodily kindness, both in public and in private. | T: “There is the case where a monk is set on bodily acts of goodwill with regard to his companions in the holy life, to their faces & behind their backs.
an6.12:1.4 | S: This warm-hearted quality makes for fondness and respect, conducing to inclusion, harmony, and unity, without dispute. | T: This is a condition that is conducive to amiability, that engenders feelings of endearment, engenders feelings of respect, leading to a sense of fellowship, a lack of disputes, harmony, & a state of unity.
an6.12:2.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant consistently treats their spiritual companions with verbal kindness … | T: “And further, the monk is set on verbal acts of goodwill with regard to his companions in the holy life, to their faces & behind their backs. This, too, is a condition that is conducive to amiability, that engenders feelings of endearment, engenders feelings of respect, leading to a sense of fellowship, a lack of disputes, harmony, & a state of unity.
an6.12:3.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant consistently treats their spiritual companions with mental kindness … | T: “And further, the monk is set on mental acts of goodwill with regard to his companions in the holy life, to their faces & behind their backs. This, too, is a condition that is conducive to amiability, that engenders feelings of endearment, engenders feelings of respect, leading to a sense of fellowship, a lack of disputes, harmony, & a state of unity.
an6.12:4.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant shares without reservation any material things they have gained by legitimate means, even the food placed in the alms-bowl, using them in common with their ethical spiritual companions. This too is a warm-hearted quality. | T: “And further, whatever righteous gains the monk may obtain in a righteous way—even if only the alms in his bowl—he does not consume them alone. He consumes them after sharing them in common with his virtuous companions in the holy life. This, too, is a condition that is conducive to amiability, that engenders feelings of endearment, engenders feelings of respect, leading to a sense of fellowship, a lack of disputes, harmony, & a state of unity.
an6.12:5.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant lives according to the precepts shared with their spiritual companions, both in public and in private. Those precepts are intact, impeccable, spotless, and unmarred, liberating, praised by sensible people, not mistaken, and leading to immersion. This too is a warm-hearted quality. | T: “And further—with reference to the virtues that are untorn, unbroken, unspotted, unsplattered, liberating, praised by the observant, ungrasped at, leading to concentration—the monk dwells with his virtue in tune with that of his companions in the holy life, to their faces & behind their backs. This, too, is a condition that is conducive to amiability, that engenders feelings of endearment, engenders feelings of respect, leading to a sense of fellowship, a lack of disputes, harmony, & a state of unity.
an6.12:6.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant lives according to the view shared with their spiritual companions, both in public and in private. That view is noble and emancipating, and delivers one who applies it to the complete ending of suffering. | T: “And further—with reference to the view that is noble, leading outward, that leads those who act in accordance with it to the right ending of suffering & stress—the monk dwells with his view in tune with that of his companions in the holy life, to their faces & behind their backs.
an6.12:6.2 | S: This warm-hearted quality makes for fondness and respect, conducing to inclusion, harmony, and unity, without dispute. | T: This, too, is a condition that is conducive to amiability, that engenders feelings of endearment, engenders feelings of respect, leading to a sense of fellowship, a lack of disputes, harmony, & a state of unity.
an6.12:7.1 | S: These six warm-hearted qualities make for fondness and respect, conducing to inclusion, harmony, and unity, without quarreling.” | T: “These are the six conditions that are conducive to amiability, that engender feelings of endearment, engender feelings of respect, leading to a sense of fellowship, a lack of disputes, harmony, & a state of unity.”
an6.12:7.2 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)


